Perhaps I was overly brief before. As I see it, nethack's alignments are
a sort of flattening of the D&D ones onto one axis.

So yes - elves and rangers are chaotic -> D&D chaotic (in slash'em elves
aren't chaotic, because they aren't "evil").

But, then there are examples of nethack's alignments corresponding to to
good/neutral/evil - chaotics don't get the murder penalty, for one.

When polymorphed into a demon, you find the idea of praying to a lawful
god repugnant (although Asmodeus (and maybe one other prince?) is
lawful). Is it fair to say that demons are evil rather than chaotic?

Holy water hurts chaotics - holiness has much more to do with good/evil
than lawful/chaotic.

Sacrificing your own race converts an altar to chaotic - again, seems to
equate chaotic with evil.

The watchmen in minetown are chaotic - I doubt that they should actually
be chaotic or evil, and assume that this is some sort of balance thing.

etc etc. The rules about killing things seem to be much more about good
vs evil to me.

The alignment section of
http://www.helsinki.fi/~vviitane/spoilers/spoil802 claims that lawful =
good, and chaotic = evil. I think it's a mixture of the two, and isn't
very consistent :)

